     "heading": "4. Account, authentication, and cloud sync",
-    "body": "The App uses Firebase Authentication. By default, Firebase may create an anonymous app session so cloud features can operate without a separate Knowly username/password.\n\nYou may optionally sign in with Google or Apple. If you do, Firebase and Knowly may process provider identifiers, Firebase Auth UID, email address if shared, display name if shared, provider type, platform, sign-in timestamps, account-linking records, and related authentication metadata. We use this to restore progress, link devices, prevent account drift, secure the service, and prevent abuse.\n\nCloud sync may store progress and app state in Firebase Firestore, including lessons, streaks, achievements, certificates, custom flashcards, your \"My mistakes\" history (the exercise, the correct answer and the answer you gave, up to 200 characters), videos you save to your favorites (the video ID and the time you saved it), daily and weekly stats, foreground usage totals, premium status metadata, RevenueCat purchase metadata, coin and Energy balances and ledgers, Peak progress, settings, language/target selections, referral state, AI dialogue memory (described in the AI features section), social feature state, and other data needed to operate requested features.\n\nIf you lose access to your progress, for example after reinstalling the App or choosing a different Google or Apple account, the App may offer to restore it with a one-time code. The code is sent by email, through our email provider (Resend), only to the email address already linked to your account. It expires after 10 minutes. We store only a protected (hashed) form of the code, together with attempt counters and recovery records used to prevent abuse.\n\nTo protect account recovery against code guessing and other abuse, the App keeps short-lived counters of recovery requests and incorrect codes. Each counter is identified only by a keyed hash (made with a secret key) of your sign-in account identifier, of the account being recovered or the email address entered for recovery, or of the IP address the request came from; we do not store the IP address itself in these counters. A counter contains only a number and timestamps, is used only to limit repeated attempts during its limit period (at most one day), and is otherwise retained only as described in the \"Retention\" section below.\n\nTo protect the service and its running costs against abuse, the App also keeps short-lived counters for nickname changes, nickname availability checks, and requests to features that call paid AI services (such as dialogue practice, conversation review, translation and voice). Each counter is identified only by a one-way hash of your account identifier or of the IP address the request came from; we do not store the IP address itself in these counters. A counter contains only a number and timestamps, is used only to limit repeated requests during its limit period (one hour or one day), and is otherwise retained only as described in the \"Retention\" section below.\n\nIf you save a video to your favourites, we store the video ID and the time you saved it in your account so your list follows you to a new device, and we keep an aggregate count of how many accounts saved each video. The count does not show who saved a video. Your list is deleted together with your account."
+    "body": "The App uses Firebase Authentication. By default, Firebase may create an anonymous app session so cloud features can operate without a separate Knowly username/password.\n\nYou may optionally sign in with Google or Apple. If you do, Firebase and Knowly may process provider identifiers, Firebase Auth UID, email address if shared, display name if shared, provider type, platform, sign-in timestamps, account-linking records, and related authentication metadata. We use this to restore progress, link devices, prevent account drift, secure the service, and prevent abuse.\n\nCloud sync may store progress and app state in Firebase Firestore, including lessons, streaks, achievements, certificates, custom flashcards, your \"My mistakes\" history (the exercise, the correct answer and the answer you gave, up to 200 characters), videos you save to your favourites (the video ID and the time you saved it), daily and weekly stats, foreground usage totals, Plus status metadata, RevenueCat purchase metadata, coin and energy balances and ledgers, Peak progress, settings, language/target selections, referral state, AI dialogue memory (described in the AI features section), social feature state, and other data needed to operate requested features.\n\nIf you lose access to your progress, for example after reinstalling the App or choosing a different Google or Apple account, the App may offer to restore it with a one-time code. The code is sent by email, through our email provider (Resend), only to the email address already linked to your account. It expires after 10 minutes. We store only a protected (hashed) form of the code, together with attempt counters and recovery records used to prevent abuse.\n\nTo protect account recovery against code guessing and other abuse, the App keeps short-lived counters of recovery requests and incorrect codes. Each counter is identified only by a keyed hash (made with a secret key) of your sign-in account identifier, of the account being recovered or the email address entered for recovery, or of the IP address the request came from; we do not store the IP address itself in these counters. A counter contains only a number and timestamps, is used only to limit repeated attempts during its limit period (at most one day), and is otherwise retained only as described in the \"Retention\" section below.\n\nTo protect the service and its running costs against abuse, the App also keeps short-lived counters for nickname changes, nickname availability checks, and requests to features that call paid AI services (such as dialogue practice, conversation review, translation and voice). Each counter is identified only by a one-way hash of your account identifier or of the IP address the request came from; we do not store the IP address itself in these counters. A counter contains only a number and timestamps, is used only to limit repeated requests during its limit period (one hour or one day), and is otherwise retained only as described in the \"Retention\" section below.\n\nIf you save a video to your favourites, we store the video ID and the time you saved it in your account so your list follows you to a new device, and we keep an aggregate count of how many accounts saved each video. The count does not show who saved a video. Your list is deleted together with your account."
