   {
     "heading": "9. Purchases, subscriptions, RevenueCat, and coins",
-    "body": "Premium subscriptions, free trials, and other in-app purchases are billed through the Apple App Store or Google Play. Knowly does not receive your payment card or bank details.\n\nWe use RevenueCat to verify subscriptions, entitlements, receipts, product IDs, transaction IDs, store, environment, purchase and expiry timestamps, cancellation or billing status, refund and chargeback signals, and related webhook events. RevenueCat may receive a Phraseman app user ID, Firebase/Auth ID, canonical stable ID, and attributes such as `phraseman_uid` so subscription and purchase state can be restored and matched to your account. To show whether you have access to paid features, the App checks your subscription status with RevenueCat each time it starts — including before you buy anything and before the optional choices in onboarding. For this check RevenueCat receives the app user ID described above and the technical store and device information needed to read store receipts. It is not used for advertising or analytics and is necessary to provide the App and restore purchases (performance of the App and our legitimate interests; see Legal bases).\n\nCoins are a virtual in-app balance earned through learning and rewards and spent inside the App (for example on Community Packs). We may store coin balances, grants, spending, refunds/reversals, pack purchases, seller credits, platform fees, reward grants, and audit logs in Firebase."
+    "body": "Phraseman Plus subscriptions, free trials, and other purchases made in the App are billed through the Apple App Store or Google Play, and Knowly does not receive your payment card or bank details. Purchases and gifts made on the Knowly website are paid through Stripe or PayPal, as described in section 14.\n\nWe use RevenueCat to verify subscriptions, entitlements, receipts, product IDs, transaction IDs, store, environment, purchase and expiry timestamps, cancellation or billing status, refund and chargeback signals, and related webhook events. RevenueCat may receive a Phraseman app user ID, Firebase/Auth ID, canonical stable ID, and attributes such as `phraseman_uid` so subscription and purchase state can be restored and matched to your account. To show whether you have access to paid features, the App checks your subscription status with RevenueCat each time it starts — including before you buy anything and before the optional choices in onboarding. For this check RevenueCat receives the app user ID described above and the technical store and device information needed to read store receipts. It is not used for advertising or analytics and is necessary to provide the App and restore purchases (performance of the App and our legitimate interests; see Legal bases).\n\nCoins are a virtual in-app balance earned through learning and rewards and spent inside the App (for example on Community Packs). We may store coin balances, grants, spending, refunds/reversals, pack purchases, seller credits, platform fees, reward grants, and audit logs in Firebase."
   },
   {
     "heading": "10. Analytics, diagnostics, device identifiers, and app health",
-    "body": "We use Firebase Analytics, Firebase Crashlytics, PostHog where enabled, and our own limited app activity/error logs to operate, secure, debug, measure, and improve the App. These records may include app events, screens/features used, purchase/paywall/referral events, AI feature events, onboarding choices, notification interactions, message or survey interactions, error context, crash logs, stack traces, app version, build number, platform, OS version, device name or model, app state, user name, Firebase/Auth IDs, stable app ID, app instance identifiers, PostHog distinct IDs where enabled, and other app metadata.\n\nThese records are not described as anonymous. Some diagnostics and analytics may be linked to your app account, Firebase/Auth ID, stable app ID, app instance, or device identifiers.\n\nSupport diagnostics: the App keeps a bounded technical action history on your device — which screens and actions were used and technical error codes from the last 24 hours, at most 200 entries (about 64 KB), stored separately for each account. It never leaves your device on its own. This history is sent to Knowly only when you explicitly submit an in-app support report: the report form tells you that it will be attached, lets you view the most recent entries before you send, and has a switch to leave the history out of that report. It is attached to that report to help us investigate the problem. It does not include message text, passwords, payment details, authentication tokens, or purchase receipts. Support reports and their attached history are deleted automatically 30 days after they are sent; they are also deleted together with your account if you delete it sooner, and you can ask us to delete them earlier through the in-app support form or by email.\n\nWhen you send an in-app report about a problem, we receive your comment, the screen and content item it concerns, the answer you gave (if any), your nickname and interface language, basic account information (such as level, streak, Premium status, and days in the App), and device information (device model, operating system and version, and screen size). Unless you switch it off on the report form, the technical action history described above is attached. These reports are deleted automatically 30 days after they are sent.\n\nWe ask for your consent before collecting non-essential product analytics (such as PostHog analytics and non-essential Firebase Analytics events). If you decline, or later withdraw consent in the App, we do not collect that non-essential analytics and do not link it to your account or identifiers. We still process a limited amount of strictly necessary information, such as crash and error diagnostics, security, and fraud-prevention signals, which we rely on to keep the App working and secure. You can change your analytics choice at any time in the App settings. We also keep a record of your analytics and AI-feature consent choices (granted or denied, when, app version and platform) so we can honour and demonstrate them.\n\nThe App does not use the Google Play Services Advertising ID and does not request it. We do not sell personal information, and we do not use any advertising identifier for third-party advertising or cross-context behavioral advertising unless this Policy, store disclosures, and any required consent are updated first."
+    "body": "We use Firebase Analytics, Firebase Crashlytics, PostHog where enabled, and our own limited app activity/error logs to operate, secure, debug, measure, and improve the App. These records may include app events, screens/features used, purchase/paywall/referral events, AI feature events, onboarding choices, notification interactions, message or survey interactions, error context, crash logs, stack traces, app version, build number, platform, OS version, device name or model, app state, your in-app nickname, Firebase/Auth IDs, stable app ID, app instance identifiers, PostHog distinct IDs where enabled, and other app metadata.\n\nThese records are not described as anonymous. Some diagnostics and analytics may be linked to your app account, Firebase/Auth ID, stable app ID, app instance, or device identifiers.\n\nSupport diagnostics: the App keeps a bounded technical action history on your device — which screens and actions were used and technical error codes from the last 24 hours, at most 200 entries (about 64 KB), stored separately for each account. It never leaves your device on its own. This history is sent to Knowly only when you explicitly submit an in-app support report: the report form tells you that it will be attached, lets you view the most recent entries before you send, and has a switch to leave the history out of that report. It is attached to that report to help us investigate the problem. It does not include message text, passwords, payment details, authentication tokens, or purchase receipts. Support reports and their attached history are deleted automatically 30 days after they are sent; they are also deleted together with your account if you delete it sooner, and you can ask us to delete them earlier through the in-app support form or by email.\n\nWhen you send an in-app report about a problem, we receive your comment, the screen and content item it concerns, the answer you gave (if any), your nickname and interface language, basic account information (such as level, streak, Plus status, and days in the App), and device information (device model, operating system and version, and screen size). Unless you switch it off on the report form, the technical action history described above is attached. These reports are deleted automatically 30 days after they are sent.\n\nWe ask for your consent before collecting non-essential product analytics (such as PostHog analytics and non-essential Firebase Analytics events). If you decline, or later withdraw consent in the App, we do not collect that non-essential analytics and do not link it to your account or identifiers. We still process a limited amount of strictly necessary information, such as crash and error diagnostics, security, and fraud-prevention signals, which we rely on to keep the App working and secure. You can change your analytics choice at any time in the App settings. We also keep a record of your analytics and AI-feature consent choices (granted or denied, when, app version and platform) so we can honour and demonstrate them.\n\nThe App does not use the Google Play Services Advertising ID and does not request it. We do not sell personal information, and we do not use any advertising identifier for third-party advertising or cross-context behavioral advertising unless this Policy, store disclosures, and any required consent are updated first."
