     "heading": "13. Text-to-speech, speech recognition, and voice features",
-    "body": "The App uses your device's built-in text-to-speech engine through expo-speech to pronounce English phrases and example sentences. Based on the current implementation, this pronunciation is generated on device and Knowly does not receive the generated text-to-speech audio. Some approved Community Packs also include pre-generated audio for their cards, created on our servers from the card text by our AI provider's text-to-speech service, as described in the Community Packs section; it does not use your voice or anything you type.\n\nWhere speech recognition, pronunciation practice, or voice dialogue features are enabled, the App may request microphone and speech recognition permissions. Depending on the feature, speech may be processed on device or by platform speech services, and a transcript or recognized text may be used by the App. If you use an AI voice dialogue feature, the transcript or recognized text may be sent to the AI provider as described in the AI features section. Do not say sensitive personal, confidential, payment, medical, legal, financial, or emergency information in voice features.\n\nWhen you answer by voice (in lessons, flashcards, pronunciation practice, or AI dialogues), the recording is kept only in the App's temporary storage on your device, so that you can play your own answer back and the App can double-check the result; it is removed when it is no longer needed, and Knowly does not receive the recording. Pronunciation is checked on your device by comparing the recognised text with the target phrase; the App may record the result (for example, the score and whether the attempt passed) as part of your learning progress and analytics, but not your voice or the recognised text. Only in AI dialogues is the recognised text of your answer sent, as described in the AI features section. On Android, speech recognition uses your device's system speech recognizer, which may be provided by Google and may process your voice under its own terms. On iPhone and iPad, speech recognition uses Apple's speech recognition service, which may process your voice on the device or on Apple's servers under Apple's own terms.\n\nOn some Android devices, the App also uses an open-source speech model (Whisper) that runs entirely on your device to double-check pronunciation. The first time you open a speaking exercise, the App downloads the model file (about 30–60 MB) once from Hugging Face, a third-party file host. As with any download, Hugging Face receives your device's IP address and basic technical request information; no audio, recognised text, or account information is sent. Your voice is never sent to Knowly or to Hugging Face for this check.\n\nSome of the libraries that power these voice features are general-purpose media and animation libraries that also support video capture and motion sensors. Because of this, on some platforms the operating system may list camera or motion permissions for the App. The App does not take photos, record video, open the camera, or read motion and fitness sensors, and it never asks you for those permissions; the only sensor input the App captures is microphone audio for the voice features described above."
+    "body": "The App uses your device's built-in text-to-speech engine through expo-speech to pronounce phrases and example sentences in the language you are learning. Based on the current implementation, this pronunciation is generated on device and Knowly does not receive the generated text-to-speech audio. Some approved Community Packs also include pre-generated audio for their cards, created on our servers from the card text by our AI provider's text-to-speech service, as described in the Community Packs section; it does not use your voice or anything you type.\n\nWhere speech recognition, pronunciation practice, or voice dialogue features are enabled, the App may request microphone and speech recognition permissions. Depending on the feature, speech may be processed on device or by platform speech services, and a transcript or recognised text may be used by the App. If you use an AI voice dialogue feature, the transcript or recognised text may be sent to the AI provider as described in the AI features section. Do not say sensitive personal, confidential, payment, medical, legal, financial, or emergency information in voice features.\n\nWhen you answer by voice (in lessons, flashcards, pronunciation practice, or AI dialogues), the recording is kept only in the App's temporary storage on your device, so that you can play your own answer back and the App can double-check the result; it is removed when it is no longer needed, and Knowly does not receive the recording. Pronunciation is checked on your device by comparing the recognised text with the target phrase; the App may record the result (for example, the score and whether the attempt passed) as part of your learning progress and analytics, but not your voice or the recognised text. Only in AI dialogues is the recognised text of your answer sent, as described in the AI features section. On Android, speech recognition uses your device's system speech recogniser, which may be provided by Google and may process your voice under its own terms. On iPhone and iPad, speech recognition uses Apple's speech recognition service, which may process your voice on the device or on Apple's servers under Apple's own terms.\n\nOn some Android devices, the App also uses an open-source speech model (Whisper) that runs entirely on your device to double-check pronunciation. The first time you open a speaking exercise, the App downloads the model file (about 30–60 MB) once from Hugging Face, a third-party file host. As with any download, Hugging Face receives your device's IP address and basic technical request information; no audio, recognised text, or account information is sent. Your voice is never sent to Knowly or to Hugging Face for this check.\n\nSome of the libraries that power these voice features are general-purpose media and animation libraries that also support video capture and motion sensors. Because of this, on some platforms the operating system may list camera or motion permissions for the App. The App does not take photos, record video, open the camera, or read motion and fitness sensors, and it never asks you for those permissions; the only sensor input the App captures is microphone audio for the voice features described above."
